      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "ἱλαρὰ δὲ εἰς τέλος ἦν",
          "english_quote": "She was cheerful to the end",
          "issue": "In this description, εἰς τέλος intensifies her cheerfulness. The English instead makes a temporal assertion about its duration, leaving the source's degree of cheerfulness unrepresented.",
          "suggested_revision": "Proposed for fresh review: “She was exceedingly cheerful.”",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "μήτι δύνη ἰσχυροτέρας ἀποκαλύψεις ὧν ἑώρακας ἰδεῖν;",
          "english_quote": "Are you able to see revelations more powerful than those you have seen[e]?",
          "issue": "The negatively oriented μήτι question expects a negative answer. An unmarked ability question weakens that force. Footnote e identifies the question as rhetorical but does not disclose its negative orientation.",
          "suggested_revision": "Proposed for fresh review: “Surely you cannot see revelations more powerful than those you have seen?” Preserve the question form and explain its expected negative answer in the note if needed.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "edition": "simplified",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": false,
        "no_added_claims": false,
        "names_and_negations": false,
        "speaker_attribution": true,
        "source_choices_and_notes": true,
        "terminology_policy": true
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "ἱλαρὰ δὲ εἰς τέλος ἦν",
          "english_quote": "She was cheerful to the end",
          "issue": "Independently compared with the Greek, this wording substitutes duration for the intensive sense of εἰς τέλος. It adds an endpoint claim and omits the emphasis on how cheerful she was.",
          "suggested_revision": "Proposed for fresh review: “She was extremely cheerful.”",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "μήτι δύνη ἰσχυροτέρας ἀποκαλύψεις ὧν ἑώρακας ἰδεῖν;",
          "english_quote": "Can you see revelations more powerful than those you have already seen[e]?",
          "issue": "The simplified question does not explicitly retain the negative expectation conveyed by μήτι. Its context suggests a rebuke, but that alone does not adequately preserve the source's negatively oriented question.",
          "suggested_revision": "Proposed for fresh review: “Surely you cannot see revelations more powerful than those you have already seen?” Keep the question rather than replacing it with the disclosed Latin prohibition.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Both editions cover all ten paragraphs and preserve the principal events, speakers, necessity of asking another, fasting instruction, promise, bodily warning, and plural rebuke. Both nevertheless require correction of the intensive cheerfulness expression and the negatively oriented question in X.8. The supplied reports support the notes' principal witness distinctions, including corrections and editorial readings; this assessment does not authenticate unseen manuscripts, editions, or handwriting. No replacement proposed here is automatically accepted."
